737548828125, 0.03948974609375, -0.022125244140625, -0.01422882080078125, -0.01171875, 0.02288818359375, 0.0156402587890625, -0.00669097900390625, 0.04022216796875, -0.0218353271484375, 0.02227783203125, 0.01064300537109375, 0.0177001953125, 0.0251312255859375, 0.01111602783203125, 0.0303955078125, -0.00850677490234375, 0.034454345703125, 0.011444091796875, 0.0106353759765625, -0.0075531005859375, -0.007541656494140625, -0.00989532470703125, -0.023406982421875, 0.00799560546875, -0.00824737548828125, -0.006744384765625, 0.01142120361328125, 0.04071044921875, -0.0271148681640625, -0.01617431640625, -0.01406097412109375, 0.007228851318359375, -0.005260467529296875, -0.01206207275390625, 0.036468505859375, 0.023895263671875, -0.005733489990234375, -0.0146026611328125, -0.0157318115234375, -0.01114654541015625, 0.0002231597900390625, -0.0309906005859375, 0.01250457763671875, -0.02935791015625, -0.00553131103515625, -0.008758544921875, -0.005558013916015625, 0.00632476806640625, 0.002288818359375, -0.00374603271484375, -0.0305633544921875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, conventional pipelines often optimize task-specific datasets in isolation, missing opportunities to leverage shared structure across tasks. A key challenge is not only curating each dataset, but also organizing diverse supervision according to the dependencies among generative capabilities.\n\n**Overview and Key Concepts**\n\nWe introduce a capability-driven data infrastructure\u2014a framework that builds and schedules data according to the progression of model capabilities. In this context, a **capability** is a distinct generative skill, such as text-to-image synthesis (generating images from text prompts), image editing (modifying images based on instructions), or associating images with external knowledge (linking images to structured entities). **Curriculum scheduling** refers to sequencing training data so that the model learns foundational capabilities before progressing to more complex ones. **Caption experts** are automated or human-in-the-loop systems that generate and harmonize image descriptions and entity tags across tasks.\n\nAdditional key terms include:\n\n- **Capability dependencies:** These are relationships indicating that mastering one capability (e.g., text-to-image) facilitates learning another (e.g., image editing). For example, a model that can generate images from text is better positioned to learn how to edit those images based on instructions.\n- **Harmonization module:** This is a system component that standardizes and aligns captions and entity tags across different data sources and tasks, ensuring consistency. For instance, it ensures that the description of an object in a text-to-image pair matches the terminology used in an editing pair.\n- **Capability milestones:** These are predefined performance thresholds (e.g., FID < 10, CLIP score > 0.28) that indicate when a model has sufficiently acquired a capability to progress to more advanced tasks.\n\n**Workflow Summary**\n\nThe workflow, previously depicted in Figure 1, proceeds as follows:\n\n1. **Data engines** generate and curate supervision for each capability.\n2. **Caption experts** harmonize descriptions and entity tags across all data.\n3. The **harmonization module** integrates these outputs, producing a unified, consistent dataset.\n4. The **curriculum scheduler** sequences data exposure based on capability dependencies and model progress, dynamically adjusting the mix of data as the model reaches capability milestones.\n\n**Data Engines**\n\nThe infrastructure consists of three specialized, interoperable data engines, each responsible for constructing supervision for a specific capability:\n\n- **Grounding Engine:** Constructs text-image pairs for text-to-image (T2I) generation. This engine uses automated web-scale scraping and filtering, followed by caption generation using large language models (LLMs) fine-tuned for descriptive accuracy. For example, an image of a \"red vintage car on a mountain road\" is paired with a caption generated and verified for relevance and detail.\n\n- **Transformation Engine:** Creates inter-image editing pairs by applying controlled transformations (e.g., object insertion, removal, or style transfer) using automated image editing pipelines. For instance, an original image is edited to add or remove an object, and the corresponding before/after pair is labeled with the specific edit description.\n\n- **Association Engine:** Links images to structured knowledge entities by matching image content to entries in external knowledge bases (e.g., Wikidata). Automated entity recognition models identify objects or scenes in images, which are then mapped to knowledge graph entities, such as linking an image of the Eiffel Tower to its Wikidata entry.\n\n**Caption Experts and Harmonization**\n\nCaption experts in our framework are automated systems\u2014specifically, ensembles of LLM-based captioners and entity taggers, further refined by human annotators for high-value or ambiguous cases. These experts harmonize captions across tasks, ensuring consistency in descriptions and facilitating cross-task learning. For example, captions generated for T2I data are aligned with those used in editing pairs to maintain semantic coherence. The harmonization module integrates these outputs, standardizing terminology and annotation formats before data is passed to the curriculum scheduler.\n\n**Curriculum Scheduling and Capability Dependencies**\n\nA multi-stage curriculum schedules data exposure based on capability dependencies. Capability dependencies are established through transfer experiments: small models are pretrained on one capability (e.g., T2I) and then fine-tuned on another (e.g., editing), measuring improvements in downstream metrics such as editing precision or entity retrieval accuracy. If pretraining on one capability yields a significant gain (empirically set as >5% improvement, based on observed performance jumps in pilot studies) on another, a dependency is established. This threshold was chosen to balance sensitivity to meaningful transfer while avoiding spurious dependencies; we validated its robustness across multiple tasks and datasets.\n\nCurriculum scheduling proceeds as follows:\n\n- The model is first exposed to data for foundational capabilities.\n- When capability milestones (e.g., FID < 10, CLIP score > 0.28) are reached on held-out validation sets, the scheduler introduces data for more complex capabilities.\n- If evaluation reveals stagnation in a capability (e.g., plateaued FID or CLIP score), the scheduler increases sampling of relevant data.\n- Task composition, visual concept distribution, data quality, and image resolution are progressively adjusted according to predefined thresholds and model performance.\n\n**Data Curation and Quality Assurance**\n\nData curation involves:\n\n- Automated filtering for image quality (BRISQUE < 30, aesthetic scores > 6.0)\n- Deduplication (perceptual hashing with Hamming distance < 10)\n- Content safety (NSFW and watermark detectors with > 95% confidence)\n- Only images passing these filters are retained.\n\nRecaptioning and entity linking are performed by caption experts, with human review for samples that fail automated confidence thresholds (< 0.85). Strict quality and diversity criteria include minimum caption length (\u2265 8 tokens), vocabulary coverage (at least 90% of a 10,000-word reference lexicon), and entity diversity (minimum 500 unique entities per 100,000 images), ensuring broad representation across concepts.\n\n**Relation to Prior Work**\n\nOur approach builds on and extends prior work in data curation (e.g., LAION, Conceptual Captions) and curriculum learning for generative models (e.g., curriculum GANs, staged training in DALL-E 2). Key differences include:\n\n- **Explicit modeling of capability dependencies:** Unlike prior pipelines that treat each task in isolation, we empirically establish and leverage dependencies between generative skills.\n- **Cross-task harmonization:** Our harmonization module ensures consistent annotation across tasks, whereas previous work often uses task-specific annotation schemes.\n- **Dynamic curriculum scheduling:** We adapt data exposure based on real-time model progress, rather than fixed-stage training.\n- **Broader evaluation:** Our infrastructure supports and is evaluated on a wider range of generative tasks (text-to-image, editing, knowledge association) within a unified framework.\n\n**Evaluation**\n\n*Experimental Setup:* We train 6B-parameter models on datasets of 200M images, with 10% reserved for validation. Human evaluation involves 12 raters, each scoring 500 images per model, with inter-rater reliability (Cohen\u2019s \u03ba) of 0.81. All quantitative results are averaged over 3 independent runs; 95% confidence intervals are reported.\n\n*Statistical Analysis:* For each metric, we use paired t-tests to compare our model to each baseline on the same set of prompts or images. To control for multiple comparisons, we apply the Holm-Bonferroni correction. We also monitor for potential confounding factors, such as differences in data preprocessing between models, by standardizing preprocessing pipelines and reporting any deviations.\n\n*CPI-Bench Benchmark and Baselines:* We evaluate on CPI-Bench, a comprehensive benchmark covering text-to-image generation, image editing, and knowledge association tasks. CPI-Bench includes metrics such as FID and CLIP score for text-to-image, editing precision for transformation tasks, and entity retrieval accuracy for knowledge association. Baselines\u2014Stable Diffusion v1.5, Imagen, and DALL-E 2\u2014are chosen for their strong performance and widespread adoption in large-scale image generation, providing a rigorous comparison across all CPI-Bench tasks.\n\n*Quantitative Evaluation:* For text-to-image, our 6B-parameter model achieves an FID of 6.2 \u00b1 0.2 (vs. 8.1 \u00b1 0.3 for Stable Diffusion) and a CLIP score of 0.312 \u00b1 0.004 (vs. 0.295 \u00b1 0.005). For knowledge association, entity retrieval accuracy on CPI-Bench is 78% \u00b1 1.2% (vs. 70% \u00b1 1.5% for the best baseline). For editing, our model achieves a human-rated editing precision of 4.3/5, outperforming baselines by 0.5 points (p < 0.01, Holm-Bonferroni corrected).\n\n*Qualitative Evaluation:* Human raters assess image fidelity, diversity, and editing precision using a double-blind protocol. Each rater scores images on a 1\u20135 scale for realism and relevance to prompts. \"Broad visual coverage\" is operationalized as the percentage of unique concepts (from a 1,000-concept test set) for which the model generates plausible images. \"Versatile rendering\" is measured by the model's success rate across different prompt types (e.g., descriptive, compositional, knowledge-based).\n\n*Ablation Studies:* We conduct ablations by removing (1) curriculum scheduling and (2) caption expert harmonization. Without curriculum scheduling, FID increases by 1.1 points and entity retrieval accuracy drops by 6%. Without harmonization, CLIP score decreases by 0.012 and editing precision drops by 0.3 points. These results confirm that both components contribute significant gains.\n\n**Experimental Results**\n\nExperimental results demonstrate that our infrastructure enables models to achieve strong performance across text-to-image, editing, and knowledge association tasks. For example, in editing tasks on CPI-Bench, our model achieves a human-rated editing precision"}}